While implementing this we realized we should add the option to `WebSocketAcceptContext` as well.
Additional API below:

```diff
public class WebSocketAcceptContext
{
     public virtual TimeSpan? KeepAliveInterval { get; set; }
+    public TimeSpan? KeepAliveTimeout { get; set; }
}
```

The reason for nullability is so we can choose the “global” value from `WebSocketOptions` instead when null. And the reason it’s not virtual when the `KeepAliveInterval` is, is due to legacy reasons where we have a `ExtendedWebSocketAcceptContext` type that implements `WebSocketAcceptContext` but it is now obsolete and we’ve moved away from that concept.

```csharp
builder.AddWebSockets(o =>
{
    o.KeepAliveTimeout = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1);
});

var app = builder.Build();
app.UseWebSockets();

app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    if (context.WebSockets.IsWebSocketRequest)
    {
        var webSocket = await context.WebSockets.AcceptWebSocketAsync(new WebSocketAcceptContext()
        {
            KeepAliveTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)
        });

        // websocket send/receive

        return;
    }

    await n(c);
});
```

